# What not to send to Docker as build context. Keeps the build fast (no node_modules
# or .git), and anything excluded here can never be copied into an image.

# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────
# backend/.env holds JWT_SECRET, the MySQL password, the Google client secret, the
# SMTP password, MIKROTIK_ENC_KEY and DEVICE_SECRET. Anyone with an image can read its
# layers (`docker history` / `docker save`), so .env must never be copied in.
# Configuration is passed at run time instead (--env-file / compose env).
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/secrets.h

# ── Never rebuild from someone else's install ───────────────────────────────
# node_modules is platform-specific; copying a Windows tree into a Linux image
# is how "it worked on my machine" happens. `npm ci` builds it in the image.
**/node_modules

# ── Runtime state, not build input ──────────────────────────────────────────
# These are volumes in the running container. Copying a dev machine's copies in
# would ship stale reports and someone else's backup data to the client.
backend/backups/
backend/reports/
backend/branding/
**/*.log
**/logs/

# ── Not runtime input for either image ──────────────────────────────────────
# Both Dockerfiles use this root context, so frontend/ source stays in (frontend/Dockerfile
# builds it). The local dist/ is left out: the image runs npm run build itself.
frontend/dist/
agent/
iot/
ops/
dev-snmpsim/

# ── Documentation, history, tooling ─────────────────────────────────────────
.git/
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
audits/
docs/
migrations/
*.md
*.mwb
*.sql
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
